source:
  site: Wikipedia
  lang: en
  title: Thebes, Egypt
  url: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Thebes%2C_Egypt
  page-id: 65811
  revision: "1371463967"
  modified: 2026-08-26T16:03:31Z
  retrieved: 2026-09-01
  license: CC BY-SA 4.0
  license-url: https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0/
  attribution: This document contains text from the Wikipedia article "Thebes,
    Egypt" (revision 1371463967), by Wikipedia contributors, used under CC BY-SA
    4.0.
+++
!!Thebes!! (Egyptian Arabic: طيبه), known to the
[[ ancient Egyptians | ancient-egypt ]] as !!Waset!!, was an
[[ ancient Egyptian city | list-of-ancient-egyptian-sites ]] located along the
[[ Nile ]] about 800 kilometers (500 mi) south of the [[ Mediterranean ]]. Its
ruins lie within the modern [[ Egyptian | egypt ]]
[[ city | list-of-egyptian-cities ]] of [[ Luxor ]]. Thebes was the main city
of the fourth Upper Egyptian [[ nome | nome-egypt ]] (Sceptre nome) and was
the capital of Egypt for long periods during the
[[ Middle Kingdom | middle-kingdom-of-egypt ]] and
[[ New Kingdom | new-kingdom-of-egypt ]] eras.

The city was close to [[ Nubia ]] and the [[ Eastern Desert ]], with its
valuable mineral resources and trade routes. It was a
[[ religious center | theban-triad ]] and the most venerated city during many
periods of ancient Egyptian history.

The site of Thebes includes areas on both the eastern bank of the Nile, where
the temples of [[ Karnak ]] and [[ Luxor | luxor-temple ]] stand and where the
city was situated; and the western bank, where a
[[ necropolis of large private and royal cemeteries and funerary complexes | theban-necropolis ]]
can be found. In 1979, the ruins of ancient Thebes were classified by
[[ UNESCO ]] as a [[ World Heritage Site ]].[[ ^1 ]]


